{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-105629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=49.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $7$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =7.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_