nt of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $\\pi $ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =\\pi .$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=6X,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,3Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=3Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,2Y,3Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}Y,\\,z=5Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,\\frac{1}{2}Y,5Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+25{z}^{2}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=\\frac{1}{5}Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,2Y,\\frac{1}{5}Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In cylindrical polar coordinates, the given half-cone has the equation\n\n$$\nz=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(x,y,z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta "] ["math" "r"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-103978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=3Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,Z,\\,3Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{r}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}$ for $z\\ge 0.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2},\\,Z\\ge 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z=r.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\,4Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{r}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,r\\ge 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(r,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=4Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3X,\\,4Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9919fafd6f3f26b1ea8999257d34730898e9c053ccc6556f4d529e9933ece59f"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{r}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "q-105725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/state.json" "file_sha256" "5561f9511be975027088d5af94670ba35cb83a199a68f4b942893b8624cb788b"} {"question" "e-8883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8883.json" "file_sha256" "2beef5b28ee27182dcf2f6ad502f871d5be1cda33f0bd8d1089f976c79218a49"} {"question" "e-8883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8883.json" "file_sha256" "2beef5b28ee27182dcf2f6ad502f871d5be1cda33f0bd8d1089f976c79218a49"} {"question" "e-8882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\:  5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8882.json" "file_sha256" "9f36f455eef55093293a4c5da5250e36009fe5d159cc64013b5dfd1a5ec391f4"} {"question" "e-8882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad\ny =5 Y, \\qquad\nz = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\nY = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nZ = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6X,5Y,4Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8882.json" "file_sha256" "9f36f455eef55093293a4c5da5250e36009fe5d159cc64013b5dfd1a5ec391f4"} {"question" "e-8884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8884.json" "file_sha256" "ebf6c5f26d8f91840a3c50951ffdeb8658478400a3f3a05b726081dc23a0fd74"} {"question" "e-8884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad\ny = 6Y, \\qquad\nz = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,\\,6Y\\,,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071762/example-8884.json" "file_sha256" "ebf6c5f26d8f91840a3c50951ffdeb8658478400a3f3a05b726081dc23a0fd74"}] "usage" {"e-8882" [] "e-8884" [] "q-105722" [] "e-8883" [] "q-103979" []} "basis" 2577 "retractions" [[17592186172153 :question/problem "$f(r, θ) = ⟨_____, r\\sin θ, r⟩, r \\ge 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z^{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + y^{2}$ for $z \\ge 0$. What is the first component of $f$?"] [17592186172160 :question/problem "$f(r, θ) = ⟨_____, 4r\\sin θ, r⟩, r \\ge 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z^{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16}$ for $z \\ge 0$. What is the first component of $f$?"] [17592186171797 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 5 \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\text{_____}, \\: 5 \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$. What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186171797 :question/worked-solution "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5 \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi}$."] [17592186171796 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 5\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: 4\\cos\\phi\\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{36}+\\dfrac{y^2}{25}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186171796 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 6X, \\qquad y =5 Y, \\qquad z = 4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 + Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1$.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad Z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6X, 5Y, 4Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 5\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 4\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}6\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}$."] [17592186171798 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 6r\\sin\\theta, \\: r \\big\\rangle, \\qquad r \\geq 0$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ above parametrizes the half-cone defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$z^2=\\dfrac{x^2}{25}+\\dfrac{y^2}{36}, \\qquad z\\geq 0$.\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186171798 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx = 5X, \\qquad y = 6Y, \\qquad z = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our half-cone, we get\n\n$$\nZ^2=X^2+Y^2,\\qquad Z\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $Z$-axis in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates. In cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation $Z = r$.\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = Z\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r, θ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5X, 6Y, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5 \\cdot r\\cos θ, 6 \\cdot r\\sin θ, Z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5r\\cos θ, 6r\\sin θ, r⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: r \\in [0,\\infty)]$ and $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black} 5r\\cos\\theta}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168122 17592186168123 17592186168124 17592186168125 17592186168126 17592186168164 17592186168165 17592186168166 17592186168167 17592186168168] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168121 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168121 :answer-field/id] [17592186168121 :answer-field/key] [17592186168121 :answer-field/type] [17592186168163 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168163 :answer-field/id] [17592186168163 :answer-field/key] [17592186168163 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
